blakely has the most difficult job at  cincinnati zoo he has to play with  babies all the time  he's never shown any aggression or  snapped if he gets upset kind of like  any mom would he just leaves the room  you are so confused  my name is don strasser and we're at the  cincinnati zoo and botanical gardens  blakely is a five-year-old australian  shepherd we got him from a rescue and i  use him in a nursery as a nanny or a  companion dog this is about you and  you're taking a nap blakely's here for  the main purpose of teaching the babies  the correct animal cues as they grow up  so when they are introduced back to  their own kind they know what  appropriate behavior is he teaches them  how to play how to interact  i can tell him no don't bite me but  i'm not an animal and i don't give the  same cues that he can so we make a great  team  smile for the kids for the camera  camera  blakely has worked with cheetahs  ocelots a talking  battered foxes a warthog  wallabies  to name a few he's working with four  baby cheetahs right now  it's been a long seven weeks for him  with these little cheetah cubs and he's  i think tired because he's on alert for  24 hours a day if they start making too  much noise he'll come and get us like  something's not right something's not  right  he's kind of like a second set of eyes  for us too a lot of times they still  recognize each other like dale the talk  and he raised when they see each other  he'll they'll still run up up front and  grunt at him and he'll kind of jump up  on the wall and it's kind of like hey  how you doing  he's beautiful thank you  yeah well i i like to think i have an  office but i'm pretty sure it's his  at new york's american museum of natural  history we are past the roosevelt atrium  through the frick building and tucked  away behind drawers of bones dr jack  sang is hard at work he's a dog guy i  have worked on dog evolution dog fossil  for about 10 years  jack's the man when it comes to dog  evolution and he says dogs might have a  shot at outliving us all  humans have been evolving for roughly 3  million years but dogs have been around  way longer and that history goes all the  way back 40 million years  it hasn't been easy they've dealt with  warming cooling the ice age  to determine how dogs are going to fare  as the world changes you have to start  in the past because dogs are  evolutionary marvels  the first dogs weren't really dogs as we  know them today they were tree dwellers  and really small  they changed to survive their legs  lengthened so they could run longer  distances and they grew a lot  during the ice age the big wolves  weighed around 140 pounds  but it's their teeth that are really the  key to their survival  their teeth were getting stronger to  deal with the larger bones and the grit  that they might get in their mouth when  they're hunting  what he's saying is that it's biological  that dogs are eating machines  think of their teeth as a basic  blueprint that goes back 40 million  years and can be adapted to any kind of  environmental change  the teeth in the back crush bone those  teeth in the middle are for cutting and  the front teeth are for puncturing  what's more dog's teeth have reinforced  surfaces  that means their teeth will stay intact  whether they're eating large bones or  chewing up carcasses  it also means dogs don't really need us  they'll be able to survive even if we're  no longer around to feed them because  they can eat pretty much anything that  comes their way  if we do go and they're left i'm sure  they'll adapt to whatever environment  that comes their way  pets  love us unconditionally and they don't  judge us  being up here and having a place for the  dogs makes all the difference in the  world  i know you're happy  i'm glad  dog mountain in the dog chapel is an  open dog park where dogs can really  connect with nature and enjoy spending  time outdoors with their with their  owners and having a place like the dog  chapel really helps people say goodbye  or also just leave a little message  about how much they loved them  it was basically started by artist  stephen hunick  he said the worst thing he'd ever heard  is it's just a dog  that's why stephen built the dog chapel  to memorialize these wonderful creatures  that have touched our lives so much  we've got people that pilgrimage from  every state and every continent  just to bask in the walls of the chapel  and leave a little message behind  they take comfort in knowing that those  pictures and those messages will be here  for years to come  i think dogs can bring out the best of  us and yeah it's it's horrific to say  goodbye but that's true of so many  losses in our lives  there are some places of reverence up  here i think for a lot of people however  they choose to recognize that  dogs are really special and it's one of  a kind place that we have here that  allows dogs to really shine and be  cherished as the wonderful little  creatures that they are  to be happy and healthy  they need to be able to run  and  maybe just maybe this is  my role in this world is to provide that  for them  my name is tc waite and i'm a dog musher  rescuing these dogs and giving them a  home is really important  we have right now 23 sled dogs on the  property  we kind of have this  motley crew of  scratch and dent sort of dogs that have  come to us from a variety of different  places  we have a group that have been rescued  we've got some from shelter we've got  some retired racing sled dogs that are  living out their golden years with us  in my mind it's a second chance for  these dogs  are you ready to hurt us  yeah  huskies love to pull a sled  it keeps them active it keeps their mind  focused this is what they were bred to  do over  generations even though they're older or  maybe they can't keep up with a race  team they still love to do it you don't  have to be the prima ballerina in order  to enjoy dance  the way that we make this work is we go  back and forth between colorado and  alaska so in the summer times we're down  south in colorado and hold down jobs and  save all that we can during the summer  so that we can put it away towards  towards the winter we manage to  transport  21 to 22 dogs over 3 000 miles each way  twice a year  i've got friends and family they might  not understand our crazy little life but  they they understand that we're doing  good things for these dogs  there is nothing like  experiencing a dog team in action  you're  literally dropped into this snow globe  of  wilderness that you can explore  and  you get to do it with your best friends  it just makes my heart sing  it's great life  i won't change it for anything  i was a model  i was always in heels and pumps and  platform shoes and i worried about my  weight  there just came a time i just said  enough you know i'm not happy anymore  i came to alaska with one dog and about  500 in my pocket because i wanted to be  a sled dog musher  i knew that life in alaska was for me  basically the moment i stepped off the  airplane  i got here and the mountains just blew  me away  it's like how some people feel when they  see the ocean  i personally don't feel isolated at all  in alaska because i'm surrounded by a  lot of dogs and a lot of family  racing is fun and i enjoy that but  there's a great need for rescue in  alaska  we take dogs that are rejected from  other teams and other kennels  they all have their own unique stories  and this is to me the most rewarding  work we can do  i'm training for the iditarod 2016.  the iditarod is the world's longest and  probably most well-known sled dog race  it's a thousand miles it starts in the  anchorage and goes to gnome and it  covers some amazing country and it goes  up on the coast off of the bering sea  to be on the sled with the dogs feels  amazing  you're part of this wild world where  there's caribou and there's moose and  you've got the sunrise you get the  sunset you get the 